    "case_id": "359",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s with a history of hypertension, NYHA stage III heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (<25%) from polysubstance abuse, and 2 prior strokes presented with bilateral scrotal and lower extremity edema for 10 days. He did not have chest pain, shortness of breath, or dyspnea on exertion but noted scrotal pain.\n\nFindings from a heart and lung examination were notable for mild wheezing. Measurements of pulse and blood pressure did not reveal alternation, and there was no paradoxical pulse or blood pressure.\n\n- Imaging:    * Chest radiograph: No acute cardiopulmonary processes    * Transthoracic echocardiogram: No pericardial effusions, left ventricular ejection fraction <25%, global hypokinesis        - ECG Findings (Figure 1-3):    * Sinus tachycardia at rate of 100    * Alternating QRS complex morphologies:      - QRS complex A: PR interval ~180ms with left axis deviation      - QRS complex B: PR interval ~120ms with normal axis and repolarization abnormalities    * Broad P-wave in II    * Increased QRS voltages in precordial leads    * Delta wave present in QRS complex B        - Laboratory Tests:    * Troponin: 0.12    * Brain natriuretic peptide: 328",
